{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 2580,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the first-ever image of a black\nhole released by the Event Horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7319,
    "dDurationMs": 3601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Telescope collaboration on April 10th\n2019"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It shows plasma orbiting the\nsupermassive black hole at the center of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15240,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy M87. The bright region shows\nwhere plasma is coming towards us and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20850,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since it's traveling near the speed of\nlight it appears brighter when coming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23910,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards us and dimmer when going away.\nThis is called relativistic beaming."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29440,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the images scientists can tell that the\nplasma is orbiting clockwise. It takes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34290,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 2 days for the plasma to complete\none orbit. Here we are looking at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39300,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole using electromagnetic waves\nwith a wavelength of 1.3 millimeters -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43649,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radio waves. This was the part of the\nspectrum used to make the image because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48510,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can show us the features close in to\nthe event horizon and it can pass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52890,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the accretion disk and\ninterstellar dust to reach our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56370,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telescopes. The supermassive black hole\nin M87 is very active meaning it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62579,
    "dDurationMs": 5011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constantly feeding on lots of matter\nfrom its glowing hot accretion disk, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67590,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it also has narrow collimated jets above\nand below it here shown in blue, thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73020,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be created by incredibly strong\nmagnetic fields. The jets extend out at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78180,
    "dDurationMs": 5579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least 5,000 lightyears and one of them\nappears to be pointing almost straight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83759,
    "dDurationMs": 3031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at us.\nRelativistic beaming is again the reason"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86790,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can see the jet coming towards us in\nthis Hubble Space Telescope image but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91170,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not the one going away.\nSince the jet is pointed almost straight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95970,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at us I'm thinking our perspective is\nalmost perpendicular to the accretion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101009,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk but let's have a look at what we\nwould see if we were looking at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105390,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different orientations. The warping of\nspace-time around the black hole means"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111210,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can see light even from parts of the\naccretion disk behind the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116420,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are lensed above and below the shadow region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121900,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not gonna lie this is so trippy I"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124350,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could stare at it for a long time. So the\nbig question most people are going to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130080,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asking is why is the image so fuzzy?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133780,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is because it's so tiny."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137600,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don't get me wrong the black hole itself is\nhuge, supermassive in fact. As measured"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142980,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from this picture the black hole has a\nmass 6.5 billion times that of our Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147860,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The actual size of its shadow is nearly\nthe size of our solar system, but located"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154170,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fifty three and a half million light\nyears away its size from our perspective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158490,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is only about 40 micro arcseconds -\nthat's the angle it makes on the sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164540,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand what that means\ntake the whole sky and divide it into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167850,
    "dDurationMs": 8370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "180 degrees. Then divide a degree into 60\narcminutes and an arcminute into 60"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176220,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arcseconds. Divide an arcsecond into a\nhundred"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184540,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and into a hundred again"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188240,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and into a hundred once more and that is the\nsize of the black hole shadow only about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194820,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "40 micro arcseconds. It puts us at the\nvery limit of what we are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199080,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technologically capable of seeing. In\nfact to get this sort of resolution due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204330,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the basic law of physics the\ndiffraction limit we needed a telescope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208320,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the size of the Earth. That being\nimpractical the scientists used eight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213750,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telescopes spread across the globe. They\nall simultaneously observed M87 and as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220530,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth rotated that helped fill in\nthe earth-sized telescope that they were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225090,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trying to simulate. But M87 was not the\nonly black hole they looked at. They also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231570,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observed the supermassive black hole at\nthe center of our Milky Way galaxy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235820,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sagittarius A* - they haven't yet release an image of this black hole, but this simulation shows what it might look like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243860,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's much closer at\naround 26,000 light years away but also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much smaller with a mass only four\nmillion times that of our Sun. It's also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252760,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less active, only periodically gobbling\nup matter, and it would have much more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256860,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variation over time with matter orbiting\nevery four to thirty minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261320,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's probably why we'll have to wait a little longer to see the black hole at the center of our galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266620,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, the striking image of the black hole in M87"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confirms beyond a shadow of a doubt that black\nholes do exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275960,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The general theory of relativity published over a hundred\nyears ago is supported again in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280640,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectacular fashion in the strongest\ngravities and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283720,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the limits of our ability to test the\ntheory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288220,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What I love about moments like this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290440,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the ridiculous\nextreme things these scientists did"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293920,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these 200 scientists in pursuit of a\ngoal that seemed impossible and yet they achieved it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300780,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to extend my personal\ncongratulations to everyone on the team"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305020,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for an amazing discovery. I'll be linking\nto all their work in the description"
    } ]
  } ]
}
